issue_md:
When using Connection.maxBodySize(0) together with Response.bodyStream() to download a large binary response, the response appears to be silently truncated at exactly Integer.MAX_VALUE (2,147,483,647) bytes.

I understand that jsoup is primarily an HTML parser rather than a dedicated large-file download tool. However, the documentation for maxBodySize(0) explicitly describes 0 as:

> an infinite amount (bounded only by your patience and the memory available on your machine)

This makes the observed behaviour somewhat unexpected. More importantly, the truncation is silent: the InputStream reaches EOF normally, so the calling application has no indication that the response was incomplete.

**Steps to reproduce**
We use the Blender example.
Compare with command line wget

```
wget "https://download.blender.org/demo/movies/Blender_reel_2013.mov"
--2026-09-06 15:44:16--  https://download.blender.org/demo/movies/Blender_reel_2013.mov
Resolving download.blender.org (download.blender.org)... 104.20.41.146, 172.66.172.236, 2606:4700:10::6814:2992, ...
Connecting to download.blender.org (download.blender.org)|104.20.41.146|:443... connected.
HTTP request sent, awaiting response... 200 OK
Length: 2516125285 (2,3G) [video/quicktime]
```
Expected file size is:  2516125285

```
				String url = "https://download.blender.org/demo/movies/Blender_reel_2013.mov";
				Connection con = Jsoup.connect(url.toString())
						.timeout(10800000) // 3 hours
			    		.method(Method.GET)
			    		.ignoreContentType(true)
			    		.maxBodySize(0)
			    		.followRedirects(false)
			    		.ignoreHttpErrors(true);

				Response res = con.execute();

				LOG.info("Content-Length: " + res.header("Content-Length"));
				LOG.info("Content-Type: " + res.contentType());

		        try (InputStream in = res.bodyStream();
	        	     OutputStream out = new FileOutputStream("/home/test/file.mov")) {

	        	    byte[] buffer = new byte[1024 * 1024]; // 1 MB
	        	    int bytesRead;

	        	    while ((bytesRead = in.read(buffer)) != -1) {
	        	        out.write(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
	        	    }
	        	}
```
Validate the resulting file on disk -> 2,0 GiB (2.147.483.647)

