issue_md:
Source tracking can impact the parse, due to how attributes are compared.

```java
String html = "<p><b><b><b><b><p>x";
for (boolean track : new boolean[] {false, true}) {
    Document doc = Parser.htmlParser().setTrackPosition(track).parseInput(html, "");
    System.out.println(track + ": " + doc.body().child(1).outerHtml());
}
```

Expected in both cases: `<p><b><b><b>x</b></b></b></p>`

Actual with tracking enabled: `<p><b><b><b><b>x</b></b></b></b></p>`

The first paragraph retains all four original `<b>` elements, but only three identical formatting elements should be active for reconstruction in the second paragraph (per the _[Noah's Ark](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#noah)_ clause). Source tracking adds internal range data to each element’s attributes; comparing that data prevents jsoup from recognising the three identical entries. `Attributes.equals()` and `hashCode()` should exclude internal attributes.

